## 13. Execution Protocol for Fable

**Phase 0 — Audit (no pixels yet).** Read the repo design report and its Vercel reference screenshots; study the inspiration library (§9) and distill it into the one-page taste document; discover and inventory the skills installed in this platform — Rohit has curated design-taste and craft skills for exactly this work — and apply the relevant ones throughout the build; extract exact tokens from the public landing site; inventory backend APIs and reusable components; map current routes/screens to keep-merge-kill with a one-line reason each. Deliver the audit as a short doc before building.

**Phase 1 — Architecture.** Three-surface IA, route map, user-journey flows, approval-gate inventory, copy deck v1.

**Phase 2 — Design system "Ink".** Tokens (color/type/space/motion/elevation, dark+light), core components, the signature element. Build as a token-driven system so themes are config. Capture tokens and component notes lightly as you build; the full reference-grade write-up (§9 bar) is a committed fast-follow after launch, not a blocker in this sprint.

**Phase 3 — Screens.** Build in the sandbox in journey order (§8). Parallelize independent workstreams (e.g., Approvals surface ∥ launch-kit screens ∥ settings) with sub-agents where available.

**Phase 4 — Integration & purge.** Wire to the tested backend read-safely; remove dead routes and the v1–v5 remnants; run the stray-brand sweep (Prime Directive 1); add redirects from any legacy paths.

**Phase 5 — Seed demo data (§14), then run the full journey walkthrough.**

**Phase 6 — Review gauntlet (§15), fix, and deliver.** Verification is live, not theoretical: drive every journey end to end with Playwright in the sandbox, screenshot each judge-relevant state, and attach the run results to the deliverables.

**Standing rules:** reuse before rebuild — backend changes are permitted per Prime Directive 2, but working capability is never discarded and the test suite never goes red; do not preserve front-end surface area out of sentiment; every gap flagged in the §16 format rather than assumed — and when a decision blocks progress or carries real risk, ask Rohit directly instead of choosing for him; maintain a running decision + justification ledger covering front-end and backend changes alike; keep the sandbox demo-able at the end of every phase.

**Tooling:** use every relevant capability available in the environment — installed skills, MCP connections, and plugins — rather than working blind. For anything touching the database, inspect and verify through the connected database MCP (e.g., Supabase) directly instead of guessing at schema or state. For live verification, run the application via Claude Code and drive it with the Playwright plugin — click through every journey, capture screenshots, and confirm flows behave as designed. All of this runs against the sandbox/dev environment only; nothing ever touches the live production application (Prime Directive 5).

**Work preservation — commit early, commit often.** Sessions can hit token limits, memory ceilings, or unexpected shutdowns; none of those may cost finished work. Commit after every coherent unit — a completed component, screen, or phase milestone — and at least every 30–45 minutes of active work, with atomic, descriptive messages, pushed to the remote sandbox branch (local-only commits don't count as safe). Maintain a terse `PROGRESS.md` state note updated at every checkpoint — current phase, what's done, what's in flight, the next three steps — so a fresh session resumes in minutes with zero rework. If context or token limits approach, stop starting new work: commit, update the state note, and end cleanly. Add any further resilience mechanisms you judge useful; losing work is the one failure with no excuse.

**Build-first session.** This sprint is build mode: code over prose. Required artifacts (ledgers, gap flags, checkpoint notes) stay terse — bullet-level, written as you go — and documentation never crowds out build time. The reference-grade Ink write-up (§9 bar) is a committed fast-follow after launch; during the sprint, just capture its raw material (tokens, component notes, decisions) lightly alongside the work.

**Build economics:** route your *own* work the same way the product routes tasks — Haiku/Sonnet for mechanical generation and refactors, Opus/Fable for architecture, IA, and final QA passes. Spend tokens where judgment lives.

